  whyNoSink: (name, s) => `Same rate without sinking anything; the ${name} throws away ${s}/min.`,
  whyFewer: (pa, name, pb) => `Same rate with fewer pieces: ${pa} against ${pb} for the ${name}.`,
  whySimpler: (name, pb, pa) => `The ${name} takes ${pb} pieces against ${pa}, but it mixes lanes that are far apart and is much harder to build. It’s only worth it when it saves more than 20% of the pieces.`,
  whyTie: name => `Tied with the ${name}; either one works.`,
  whyNone: 'No design fits this shape.',
  bpTitle: 'Blueprints I have',
  bpLead: 'When one of your blueprints has exactly the right outputs and enough inputs, the tool prefers it.',
  bpAdd: 'Add', bpEmpty: 'No blueprints yet.', bpRemove: (n, m) => `Remove ${n}→${m} blueprint`,
  bpInAria: 'Blueprint inputs', bpOutAria: 'Blueprint outputs',
  tipBp: 'Blueprint Designer blueprints you already built. Each counts as a single piece, because you just paste it. A spare input stays empty with no harm; a spare output unbalances the split, so the output count has to match.',
  lbWhatTitle: 'What it is',
  lbWhat: 'A load balancer is a set of splitters and mergers that takes N belts and delivers M belts at exactly the same rate, no matter how much each input carries. There is no “balancer” piece in the game: you build it from regular pieces.',
  lbHowTitle: 'How it works',
  lbHow: 'A splitter divides evenly among the outputs that accept items, into 2 or 3. Chaining splitters cuts a belt into any product of 2 and 3 (2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 9, 12…). A merger joins up to 3 belts, taking from each in turn. A matrix uses both: each input is cut into M equal parts and each output gets one part from every input, so every output adds up to the same.',
  lbWhyTitle: 'Why use one',
  lbWhy: 'A manifold balances itself across machines, but not across lines: if one destination line gets 455 and another 300, the machines on the second starve forever. A balancer makes every line carry the same share of the total. It’s also how you split a belt into 5 or 7, which a splitter can’t do directly.',
  lbNotTitle: 'When you don’t need one',
  lbNot: 'If each source line already covers a destination, connect them directly. If the shortfall is small, a split donor line or a Smart Splitter with overflow does it with fewer pieces. The alternatives above compare this for your case.',
  lbBuildTitle: 'How to build your own',
  lbBuild1: 'Write M as a product of 2 and 3, the 2s first (6 = 2 × 3: 3 splitters, while 3 × 2 takes 4). If you can’t (5, 7, 10…), use the next number that works and choose: loop or sink.',
  lbBuild2: 'On each input, build the tree: one splitter for the first factor, then one splitter on each output for the next factor.',
  lbBuild3: 'For each output, take the part with the same number from every input and join them with a merger. With more than 3 inputs, chain mergers.',
  lbBuild4: 'Loop: the extra parts go back to a merger before the first splitter (3 or more parts: cascaded mergers). Sink: they go to the Awesome Sink.',
  lbBuild5: 'Check the belt on every segment. If the total fits on one belt, the funnel (merge everything, then split) uses far fewer pieces.',
  catTitle: 'Design catalog',
  catLead: 'The design families the tool builds, including 5, 7 and other counts a splitter can’t reach directly. Open an example in the generator.',
  dsMatrix: 'Each input becomes M parts; each output joins one part from every input. Handles the rate of several full belts. Works when M is a product of 2 and 3.',
  dsFunnel: 'Merges every input onto one belt and splits it into M. Far fewer pieces, but everything runs on a single belt: it works if the total fits on it.',
  dsMatrixLoop: 'Matrix for 5, 7, 10…: each tree splits into the next count a splitter can reach and the extra parts loop back to the input. Exact split, nothing lost; the loop belt carries more than the input.',
  dsMatrixSplitLoop: 'For a full belt: each input first goes through a splitter into 2, and each half gets its own looped tree. The loop carries only a bit more than half an input, so it fits on the belt and nothing bottlenecks. It’s the 14-piece 1→5 and the 24-piece 1→7 the community uses.',
  stHalves: (n, half) => `Split ${n > 1 ? 'each input' : 'the input'} into 2 with a splitter (${half}/min on each half). Each half gets its own looped tree, and a half-input loop fits on the belt.`,
  dsFunnelLoop: 'Funnel with a loop: the cheapest way to split into 5 or 7, if the total plus the return fits on one belt.',
  dsMatrixSink: 'Matrix that splits into more parts and sends the extras to the Awesome Sink. No loop and easy to build, but it throws items away.',
  dsFunnelSink: 'Funnel that splits into more parts and sinks the extras. The simplest way to get 5 or 7 at low rates, when losing items doesn’t matter.',
  catExample: (n, m) => `Open ${n}→${m}`,
